cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or the released results, please cite the paper (anonymous submission to CVPR 2027)."
title: "Parallel Motion as an Exact Counterfactual: How Much Geometry Do Feed-Forward 3D Models Use for Moving Objects?"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Anonymous Authors"
version: "1.0.0"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - 3D reconstruction
  - feed-forward reconstruction
  - dynamic scenes
  - multi-view geometry
  - probing
abstract: >-
  An object that moves parallel to the camera baseline by a fraction s of it looks, in both images,
  exactly like a static object 1/(1-s) times as far away. The slope of a model's log-depth error on
  x = -log(1-s) is the share of geometry the model uses for the object. This repository holds the
  notebook that ran every experiment, a tested package with the model-independent code, the
  registration record and all outputs of the run reported in the paper.
